 in episodes  well no but we have been dealing with it  as a culture and as a fandom long enough  and loudly enough that I I think the  best statement that they could make  would be no statement just let it be  just have her there being the doctor in  you know with her own nuances every  version of the doctor has had its own  nuances so I mean I'm not saying she has  to do it exactly the way anyone else did  it but not just don't bring it up the  change happen it's the thing that  happened why do we have to bog anything  down with that and again I know that  there are some people who really feel  that this needs to be justified in some  way like there needs to be some  explanation as to why this happened I've  seen even just just from my own comments  section all sorts of things get thrown  out everything from I think you know  Bill's tier somehow affected the doctor  and that's why it's gonna become a woman  I've seen people say that they think  because of seeing the more misogynistic  tendencies of the first doctor in twice  upon a time maybe the doctor want to be  a woman more and things like that and I  don't think there's anything wrong with  these theories I'm certainly not going  you're wrong for thinking that and it's  fine if that's your head can and go with  it I just I think there are better uses  of the show's time than to actually eat  up time addressing that stuff  specifically because basically what they  be doing is they would be making  responses to arguments from over a year  ago anything that they do in the show  that's like and this is why the doctor  is a woman now first of all is not going  to actually quell anyone who is not okay  with the doctor being a woman nothing  that you do is actually going to quell  people who are fundamentally not okay  with it  so all you're doing is this weird  retrograde thing where you do like an  official response to an argument that  feels like it should be winding down  instead of heating up again and in  general  I just think that about as much emphasis  should be put on the fact that the  doctor is now a woman as was previously  put on the fact that the doctor was a  man which is to say virtually none even  going back into the classic era there  are not a lot of stories where the fact  that the doctor is male actually has  direct influence on the narrative or is  even like touched upon as a thing to  talk about it did not happen very often  and I don't think it should happen very  often now that we've done the opposite  because then you just spend the entire  time she's there repeatedly trying to  justify the decision you made look the  decision was made some people didn't  like it some people will never like it  some people may turn around on it we'll  see but you're not gonna tell any good  stories if you're eating up all your  time and you keep coming back to trying  to justify it a decision that was  already made and already had its impact  it would have been like having Colin  Baker's doctor constantly keep bringing  up why it is he's wearing this huge  massive colorful coat which again is  something I hate but taking time out of  stories to justify it would not have  made it any better although that said I  guess Matt Smith did continually  justified like wearing a bowtie Oh God  look Christian Bell if you even had an  inkling to do a joke of like oh I've got  boobs now boobs are cool don't please  don't  and that's another sort of thing I feel  like at this point we need to just roll  with the idea that gender doesn't have a  ton of meaning within Timelord Society  now that's not saying that it has  absolutely no meaning to the doctor he  seems to understand gender well enough  as far as it is a distinction for his  companions if nothing else so it's not  like he's above gender or whatever but I  think at this point it's just best to  start rolling with the idea that they  don't get hung up on it like as a  species it's just it's it's like being  blonde or brunette why are you making a  big deal out of that because what I'm  really looking forward to what I'm  really excited to see is not how is a  woman doctor going to be rather than  just how is the nude  dr. going to be I would be equally as  excited and interest if they cast a man  if they catch anybody I want to see  what's next I don't need to see what's  next for a woman I don't care at this  point look I'm in I'm not going away  like I said I've got concerns I've got  reservations I'm not gonna pretend like  they vanished into nothing but my  enthusiasm is growing and largely  overshadowing my concerns and my  enthusiasm is for just finding out the  kinds of adventures we get the kind of  chemistry that she's gonna have with her  companions what kind of villains is she  got to face what kind of planets is she  gonna go to what's the sonic screwdriver  look like I don't really care how being  a woman changes her and I kind of think  it shouldn't because the more that you  decide that oh the doctor being a woman  means so much changes the more  importance you assign to gender and I'm  not saying that in our society gender  isn't important but again we see the the  whole thing seems to be leaning towards  it's not particularly important to the  Timelords  so that's us imposing something and  importance onto a character when it's  not important to them and that just  seems like a waste of energy a waste of  emphasis I don't think it's necessary  for the doctor to become a crusader for  women's causes not that I'm saying it  should be avoided either the doctor  should be a crusader for justice as the  doctor has always been in every  incarnation but I don't think that it is  necessary for the doctor to suddenly  become distinctly feminine icon in a way  that is different from how the character  has been portrayed before because I  think just the fact that the doctor is a  woman now isn't it says it that is  enough I personally am not particularly  interested in how the 